@node Overview
@chapter Overview

A large mixture-of-experts (@dfn{MoE}) checkpoint does not fit in RAM, so it is
read from storage while it runs. That makes decoding a storage problem, and
storage cares enormously about @emph{how} you ask.

On the machine this was developed on --- an i5-1235U laptop with 7.6@tie{}GB of
RAM and no GPU --- the same NVMe device delivers 0.099@tie{}GB/s at queue depth
one and 3.291@tie{}GB/s at depth sixteen. A factor of thirty-three, from nothing
but concurrency.

@command{potatomaxx} attacks the cost of those reads three ways, and
@emph{measures} each rather than asserting it:

@enumerate
@item
@b{Layout}: reorder experts on disk so the ones that fire together are adjacent,
turning scattered slice reads into contiguous runs. @xref{Layout}.
@item
@b{Precision}: store each expert at its own bit width, chosen from measured
quantisation error and how often the expert is actually used. @xref{Precision}.
@item
@b{Lookahead}: predict which experts a layer will select before its router has
run, because you cannot queue reads you have not predicted. @xref{Lookahead}.
@end enumerate

It is not an inference engine and does not try to be. It has no attention, no KV
cache and no sampler. It makes a model cheaper to read; something else runs it.

@cindex dependencies
The program has no dependencies beyond the Rust standard library. The GGUF
parser, the command line, the io_uring implementation and the JSON reader are all
written here, deliberately: every dependency widens the surface exposed to an
untrusted model file.

@node Invoking potatomaxx
@chapter Invoking potatomaxx

@example
potatomaxx @var{command} [@var{options}]
@end example

Run @samp{potatomaxx help} for the full option list. The commands divide into
measurement, the layout pipeline, and the streaming path.

@section Measurement

@table @command
@item probe
Measure this device's read bandwidth surface --- request size against queue
depth --- using @code{O_DIRECT} so the figures describe the device and not the
page cache. Writes JSON the other commands cost against.

@item kio
Compare kernel I/O paths: synchronous @code{pread}, a thread pool, and io_uring
in both batch and sliding-window form, with and without @code{RWF_DONTCACHE}.
Reports page-cache residency alongside bandwidth, and recommends an engine from
the measurement rather than from a preference. @xref{Kernel I/O}.

@item synth
Write a small synthetic MoE checkpoint and routing trace, so the whole pipeline
runs in seconds without downloading a model.

@item inspect @var{model}
Report a checkpoint's MoE structure: experts per layer, slice size, precision,
and which tensors carry the expert axis.
@end table

@section The layout pipeline

@table @command
@item analyse
Score the existing expert order against an optimised one, per layer, costed
against a measured surface. Reports plainly when the gain is not worth rewriting
a file for.

@item plan
Write a plan. It is plain text on purpose: it authorises rewriting a
multi-gigabyte file, so a reviewer should be able to read exactly what will
happen first.

@item pack
Apply a plan, emitting a new GGUF.

@item verify
Prove the result holds exactly the original weights. @xref{Layout}.
@end table

@section The streaming path

@table @command
@item predict
Compare router-lookahead predictors on a trace, fitted on a prefix and scored on
held-out tokens.

@item build-store
Requantise per expert into a native @file{.pmxstore}. @xref{Precision}.

@item bench
Replay a trace against a store, measuring the expert-fetch path.
@end table

@node Layout
@chapter Layout

@cindex relabelling
@cindex permutation, expert
Permuting the expert axis is a @dfn{relabelling}. If new slot @var{j} holds old
expert @code{perm[@var{j}]}, and the router's weight rows are permuted by the
same @code{perm}, then the logit computed for slot @var{j} is exactly the logit
the original model computed for expert @code{perm[@var{j}]}. Top-@var{k} selects
the same real experts, from the same bytes. The function computed is unchanged,
bit for bit.

The GGUF specification permits the file-level half of this: tensor data is
located only through the @code{offset} in its @code{tensor_info}, physical order
is unconstrained, and inter-tensor padding is explicitly allowed. A repacked file
is therefore a drop-in GGUF.

@cindex verify
@command{potatomaxx verify} proves it afterwards, reading both files in full and
comparing every expert slice. It is strict: flipping four bytes anywhere fails
it, and continuous integration asserts that it does.

Layout is the @emph{secondary} lever, and saying so matters. @xref{Findings}.

@node Precision
@chapter Precision

@cindex mixed precision, per expert
Per-expert precision cannot be expressed in GGUF at all: a tensor carries one
@code{ggml_type}, and a MoE layer's experts share a tensor. Giving expert seven
three bits and expert eight five bits therefore needs a container defined here,
which is what @file{.pmxstore} is.

Bits follow @b{access frequency and tier cost}, not structural role. An expert's
contribution to decode time is proportional to how often it is read and to which
tier serves it; its contribution to quality loss is proportional to how often it
is read and how badly it quantises. Minimising expected loss under a movement
budget therefore says something the usual formulation does not: spend fewer bits
where bytes are most expensive and least often needed.

@cindex error, measured
The error term is @emph{measured} --- the round-trip error of actually
quantising that expert's weights at each candidate precision --- not an analytic
proxy. An earlier version used @code{4^-bits}, which against an F16 baseline
reported such enormous loss for any quantisation that the allocator silently
refused every change while reporting success.

@cindex router, not requantised
@cindex expert shift
@b{Routers are never requantised.} Quantisation error in a router perturbs expert
@emph{selection} --- the ``expert shift'' problem --- which would invalidate the
very trace the plan was derived from and silently change which experts the model
uses. There is no option to do otherwise.

@node Lookahead
@chapter Lookahead

A device's bandwidth depends far more on how many reads are in flight than on
anything a file layout controls. But you cannot have eight reads outstanding for
experts you have not been told about, and the router for layer @var{n} runs only
after layer @var{n-1} finishes. Prediction buys that time, and is therefore the
mechanism by which the largest available speedup is claimed.

A wrong prediction is worse than none: it spends bandwidth @emph{and} still
stalls. So @command{predict} reports precision as well as recall, and
@command{bench} charges every prefetch as the real read it is.

The predictors here are training-free, using only routing history a running
engine already has. Trained pre-attention routers do considerably better, and the
documentation says so rather than implying parity.

@node Kernel I/O
@chapter Kernel I/O

@cindex io_uring
@cindex RWF_DONTCACHE
@cindex cachestat
Five kernel interfaces matter to this workload. Each is probed at runtime rather
than inferred from a version string, because distributions backport, containers
lie, and @code{RWF_DONTCACHE} additionally requires the filesystem to have opted
in.

@table @code
@item io_uring
Queue depth with one syscall per batch instead of one per read. How it is driven
matters more than using it: see @ref{Findings}.

@item RWF_DONTCACHE
Read through the page cache but drop the range on completion. Streaming a
forty-gigabyte store otherwise fills the page cache with data that will not be
reused, evicts every other process's working set, and makes reclaim the
bottleneck.

@item MADV_HUGEPAGE
The resident hot set is read on nearly every token, so its page-table walks are
on the critical path.

@item MADV_RANDOM
Readahead bets the next block is wanted. For expert fetch that is a losing bet: a
router decides, not address order.

@item cachestat
Ask how much of a range is actually resident, rather than believing a cache plan
is being honoured.
@end table

@cindex kernel, why not in the
An inference engine cannot go in the kernel, and should not. Floating point is
prohibited there; the @code{kernel_fpu_begin} escape hatch disables preemption,
so a matrix multiply inside it would create unbounded non-preemptible sections.
And a model is policy, where the kernel's rule is mechanism only. The sanctioned
route for application-specific policy in a hot path is BPF. The distribution's
@file{docs/KERNEL.md} sets this out at length.

@node Measured or not
@chapter What is measured, and what is not

This distinction is the point of the program, so it is stated before any
feature.

@table @asis
@item Measured
The bandwidth surface, the engine comparison, queue-depth scaling, predictor
recall on held-out tokens, cache policy against the offline optimum, and
per-expert quantisation error. Also that @command{verify} proves byte-identical
weights, which is enforced by tests including a tamper control.

@item Computed
Weight-movement speedup and expert-fetch throughput. These are arithmetic over
measured constants and named assumptions, not results, and are labelled as such
where they are reported.

@item Unknown
The speedup on @emph{your} model. That is what @command{analyse} is for.

@item Not measured
End-to-end generated tokens per second. There is no attention, KV cache or
sampler here, so @command{bench} reports a ceiling on decode rate rather than a
decode rate. Quality is measured as round-trip error, which is not perplexity: a
plan that looks cheap by that measure still needs a real evaluation.
@end table

@node Findings
@chapter Findings that contradicted the design

Kept because they are the most useful output, and each is now a regression test.

@table @asis
@item Layout is the secondary lever
The original design argued for co-activation layout as the primary win.
Measurement disagreed: once a layer's top-@var{k} reads are issued concurrently,
reordering buys 1.1 to 1.6 times, and only for slices in a particular size band.
Queue depth is worth six to thirty-three times, and no layout can influence it.

@item Fixed groups read whole are a net loss
Over-reading a two-megabyte group costs more than coalescing saves above roughly
256@tie{}KiB slices.

@item Cost-awareness has a precise limit
A frequency-and-cost-aware cache reaches 90.7% of the offline optimum against
LRU's 80.9% on skewed routing. But its key contains @code{cost / size}, so at a
fixed bandwidth that term is constant and it degenerates to plain LFU --- which
is @emph{worse} than LRU. Cost-awareness pays across storage tiers, not within
one.

@item Prefetching is not free
Every prediction is a real read. Throughput peaks near top-@var{k} and then falls
while recall keeps climbing. In one configuration plain on-demand LRU beat every
prefetch setting.

@item A budget relative to the baseline is degenerate
Once sensitivity is measured the baseline @emph{is} the reference and its error is
zero, so any multiple of it permits nothing while still reporting success.

@item How io_uring is driven matters more than using it
Submitting a batch and waiting for all of it drains the ring between batches, so
the device idles in the gap; that form peaked at queue depth eight and then
declined. A sliding window gained 148% at the same depth. And it still lost to a
thread pool on this machine, because one ring submitted and reaped from a single
thread is not enough where the block path is virtualised.

@item RWF_DONTCACHE is slower, and worth it
It measured 49% slower and is not a throughput optimisation. It declines to
retain pages nothing will read again, so the cost of a streaming read falls on
this process rather than on everything else on the machine.
@end table

@node Security
@chapter Security

@cindex threat model
The threat model is narrow, which makes it easy to state. @command{potatomaxx}
parses attacker-controlled files: a checkpoint is downloaded from a public hub by
someone who did not write it, and its header is read with offset arithmetic.

That is the code path which, in other implementations of this format, produced
CVE-2026-27940 (an integer overflow to an undersized heap allocation, then a
controlled overflow, itself a bypass of an earlier fix) and CVE-2026-7482, a
heap out-of-bounds read from inflated tensor dimensions that leaked process
memory.

The contract here is absolute: @b{for any input at all, parsing returns success
or an error --- never a panic, never a hang, never a read outside the file.} A
dedicated suite enforces it with random inputs, every single-byte mutation of a
valid header, truncation at every length, inflated counts and dimensions,
unaligned and out-of-range offsets, and invalid UTF-8.

Every component forbids @code{unsafe} code except three, each for a stated reason
that safe Rust cannot express: SIMD intrinsics, page-aligned buffers for
@code{O_DIRECT}, and raw syscalls with shared io_uring mappings. In each, the
portable implementation is authoritative and the optimised path is tested against
it.

@xref{Top}, and the distribution's @file{SECURITY.md}, for how to report a
problem.

